WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.283
The bootstrap: when the formula runs out.

2
00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:06.616
Bradley Efron introduced the bootstrap in 1979.

3
00:00:06.896 --> 00:00:15.493
The idea: you can’t draw new samples from the population, but you can draw from your sample, which is the best estimate of the population you have.

4
00:00:15.773 --> 00:00:26.952
Resample your n questions with replacement, recompute the statistic, repeat a few thousand times, and the spread of the recomputed values approximates the sampling distribution.

5
00:00:27.232 --> 00:00:29.259
The percentile interval takes the 2.

6
00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:30.989
5th and 97.

7
00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:33.317
5th percentiles of those values.

8
00:00:33.597 --> 00:00:36.115
For a plain pass rate the bootstrap adds little.

9
00:00:36.395 --> 00:00:44.672
Miller notes that bootstrapping is common in the eval literature but unnecessary for a simple mean, since the CLT already applies.

10
00:00:44.952 --> 00:00:53.357
Your bootstrap interval for prompt A will land within a point or so of the CLT interval, which makes it a good check that your code is right.

11
00:00:53.637 --> 00:01:07.589
The bootstrap earns its keep when the statistic has no tidy formula, and in AI engineering that is often: Cost per successful task: total spend divided by number of passes, a ratio of two random quantities.

12
00:01:07.869 --> 00:01:13.309
Median or 95th-percentile latency, where the CLT for a mean doesn’t apply.

13
00:01:13.589 --> 00:01:18.752
Agreement between a judge and human labels, or a true positive rate on a small subset.

14
00:01:19.032 --> 00:01:25.837
Clustered data, where you resample whole clusters (all questions about one document) instead of single questions.

15
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:27.717
Two rules keep it honest.

16
00:01:27.997 --> 00:01:36.147
Resample the unit that was randomized: questions, or clusters of questions, never individual model runs of the same question.

17
00:01:36.427 --> 00:01:46.453
And when comparing two systems, resample question indices once and keep both systems’ scores for each question together, which is the paired bootstrap you’ll write below.

18
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:50.893
Fix the random seed so that the interval in your report can be reproduced.
